From 89c165531491fbb0a6811faa5b49cdbffeea0bd5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ferdinand Vanmaele <info@fvanmaele.de>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 11:51:38 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [clangd] Index navigation: header shards keep their command,
 checked against the includer's

A header's shard dropped the compile command it was indexed with
(BackgroundIndex::update reset it for every file but the main one), so
shardCommandMatches had nothing to compare and always matched. After a
-D change, navigation from the index in a header opened directly answered
from the #ifdef branches of the old flags, in every session, until the
header's content changed (S23 case 3).

A header's shard now keeps the command of the TU whose indexing stored it.
Navigation from a header (a shard whose node is not a TU) answers from the
index only when that command has the flags of the current command of the
header's recorded includer (BackgroundIndex::knownIncluderOf, after the
stored shards are loaded): the same directory and arguments apart from
the source file and the -o / -MF / -MT / -MQ values, which differ between
sources of one target (headerShardCommandMatches). A shard stored without
a command does not match. Otherwise the AST answers, and the log says why.

Also moves locateIncludeFromShard's comment, which sat above
shardCommandMatches, back to its function.

ClangdTests 1434/1434 (new: HeaderShardKeepsItsIncludersCommand,
HeaderShardOfOtherFlagsDoesNotAnswer; CmdLineHash now expects the
header's command). S23 case 3 through LSP: the AST answers right in both
sessions after the change; with the flags unchanged the index answers
from the header (0.28 s).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
